WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.510
Integrity under pressure: a simple way to decide.

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.230
This is the video for module one of Ethics and Accountability at Work.

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:20.400
Ethics is the set of standards we use to decide what is right and fair when our choices affect other people: customers, colleagues, your employer, the members of your group.

4
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:31.150
Accountability is the other half. It means being willing to answer for what you said you would do, what you actually did, and what happened, including your mistakes.

5
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:45.240
Ethics is not the same as law or custom. Promising delivery on Friday when you know it will be Tuesday may be legal, but it is not honest. And things everybody does are not automatically right.

6
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:57.090
Integrity means your words and actions match, whether or not anyone is watching. A cashier with integrity counts the money the same way when the manager is away.

7
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:04.550
Accountability is something you can practise. First, own it: say, that was my responsibility.

8
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.400
Second, explain the facts, not excuses.

9
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.230
Third, repair what you can.

10
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.370
And fourth, prevent a repeat. A person who only owns, but never repairs, is sorry but not accountable.

11
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.210
For example, Amaka is a sales assistant in a Lagos pharmacy shop.

12
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.740
Her monthly target is 2,000,000 naira.

13
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:40.790
By the 30th she has reached about 1,850,000 naira. A colleague suggests recording next week's expected clinic order as this month's sale.

14
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:49.260
In plain words, that is recording a sale that has not happened. The owner would order the wrong stock and pay a bonus nobody earned.

15
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:57.760
So Amaka reports 1,850,000 naira, with a note that a confirmed clinic order is expected next week.

16
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:15.120
Most wrong choices are not made by bad people. They are made by ordinary people under pressure: a boss who wants a result, a relative asking a favour, a colleague saying we all do it. Watch for soft words like settle or adjust the figures.

17
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:23.260
When you are unsure, run three quick tests. The rules test: does this break a law, a policy or our bye-laws?

18
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.480
The harm test: who could be harmed, now or later?

19
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:34.360
The daylight test: would I be comfortable if this appeared on the group WhatsApp, with my name on it?

20
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:44.010
Gifts are part of our culture. A token anyone could accept openly, like a calendar or a pack of biscuits, is usually fine.

21
00:02:45.260 --> 00:03:00.070
For example, Ngozi buys supplies for a school in Enugu. A supplier whose contract is up for renewal sends a hamper with an envelope of 50,000 naira. She would not be comfortable if the proprietor saw it. That is her answer.

22
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:09.570
She returns the envelope with a polite note and tells the proprietor in writing. Cash from someone who wants something from you is almost always a bribe.

23
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:21.470
Some situations are dilemmas, where two things you care about pull apart. For example, Funmi is financial secretary of a 30-member cooperative in Ibadan.

24
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:31.180
Her friend Bisi has not paid for three months and asks to be marked as paid. Funmi pauses: friendship against fairness to 29 other members.

25
00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:40.220
She gets the facts. Bisi owes 15,000 naira, and a false entry could let her take a loan she is not entitled to.

26
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:50.020
She lists three options, not two: mark her paid, refuse and say nothing, or refuse and help Bisi ask for a written payment plan.

27
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:58.270
She chooses the third, and tells Bisi kindly: I can't write what didn't happen, but I'll come with you to ask for a plan.

28
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:08.100
She follows up. The executive approves a plan, Funmi records each payment exactly, and Bisi clears the balance in six months.

29
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:20.570
Doing right can carry real costs, so protect yourself. Keep dated notes and save messages. Raise concerns in writing with the right person, and find a trusted ally.

30
00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:29.490
Never confront a powerful person alone. If anyone threatens you, leave, keep the evidence, and seek help from people who can protect you.

31
00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:46.570
Your practice. Write down three soft words used around you, and what they really describe. Write two polite refusal scripts and practise them aloud. Then work one real dilemma through the five steps, with a facts column and an assumptions column.

32
00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.080
Now continue with the lessons below.
